# The central Alertmanager's config, committed in the clear
# (fort-layers#3's shape, at the hub): nothing in the default is a
# secret, so a route change is a readable PR diff. The operator reads the
# Secret alertmanager-<name> — here alertmanager-central — key
# alertmanager.yaml.
#
# The default route delivers to a receiver with no integration: alerts
# land in this Alertmanager's UI and in Grafana (Alerting → the
# Alertmanager Central datasource), and a fresh hub reconciles green with
# no hand-written file. Grouped by alert name and cluster, so a
# fleet-wide alert is one notification per fort and a fort's burst is
# one. A notifier is an opt-in edit of this file plus one secret:
#
#   1. The secret: one key, `url`, encrypted under this fort's own sops
#      rule (the hub cluster is the only reader):
#        flux/apps/{{ .Fort }}/monitoring-central/alertmanager-webhook.sops.yaml
#        (kind Secret, name alertmanager-webhook, namespace
#        monitoring-central, stringData: url: <the webhook URL>)
#      and list it in the stack's kustomization.
#   2. The mount: add to the Alertmanager CR (alertmanager-central.yaml)
#        spec.secrets: [alertmanager-webhook]
#      The mount is part of the opt-in, not the default: a named-but-
#      missing Secret blocks the pod.
#   3. The receiver — every common target speaks webhook:
#        receivers:
#          - name: notify
#            webhook_configs:
#              - url_file: /etc/alertmanager/secrets/alertmanager-webhook/url
#                send_resolved: true
#      and route.receiver: notify. Discord is discord_configs with
#      webhook_url_file, Slack is slack_configs with api_url_file — the
#      same file, the same mount.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: alertmanager-central
  namespace: monitoring-central
type: Opaque
stringData:
  alertmanager.yaml: |
    route:
      receiver: none
      group_by: [alertname, cluster]
      group_wait: 30s
      group_interval: 5m
      repeat_interval: 4h
    receivers:
      - name: none
